<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- English (United States) strings for MacroPolo.admx. -->
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0"
                           schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">

  <displayName>Macro Polo</displayName>
  <description>Group Policy settings for the Macro Polo add-in for Word and Excel.</description>

  <resources>
    <stringTable>

      <string id="IronJay">iron-jay</string>
      <string id="MacroPolo">Macro Polo</string>

      <string id="AutoShow">When to show the macro status banner</string>
      <string id="AutoShow_Explain">Controls whether the macro status banner appears on its own when a document is opened.

If you enable this setting, choose one of:

  - Only when the ribbon button is pressed. The banner never appears by itself. This is the default.

  - When the document contains macros. The banner appears automatically for any document with macro code, and stays out of the way otherwise.

  - For every document. The banner appears on every document, including those with no macros at all.

If you disable or do not configure this setting, users may choose for themselves, and the banner only appears when the Macro check button on the Home tab is pressed.

Note that this only controls when the banner appears unprompted. The ribbon button always works, whatever this is set to.</string>
      <string id="AutoShow_Never">Only when the ribbon button is pressed (default)</string>
      <string id="AutoShow_WhenMacrosPresent">When the document contains macros</string>
      <string id="AutoShow_Always">For every document</string>

      <string id="Logging">Enable diagnostic logging</string>
      <string id="Logging_Explain">Writes a diagnostic log to %LOCALAPPDATA%\Macro Polo\macro-polo.log.

If you enable this setting, the add-in records what it does for each document it inspects, which is useful when a machine is reporting something unexpected.

If you disable or do not configure this setting, nothing is written.

The log names every document checked, so it is worth turning off again once the question has been answered.</string>

    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="AutoShow">
        <dropdownList refId="AutoShow" noSort="true" defaultItem="0">Show the banner:</dropdownList>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
